# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  What git is told to leave alone, and why.
#
#  Every entry is something this project actually produces, or something this project must never
#  contain. `start.bat` creates the Python environment, the window's dependencies and the interface
#  bundle; `npm run pack` and `npm run dist` produce the packaged app. None of it is source, and all
#  of it is reproduced by running one of those, so none of it belongs in a commit.
#
#  Nothing has to be committed for the application to run from a clone: `start.bat` does every step
#  it finds missing, which is the point of it.
#
#  To find out which rule caught a path, and from which line of this file:
#
#      git check-ignore -v <path>
#
#  To commit something one of these rules is holding back, name it with `!` below rather than
#  reaching for `git add --force`: an exception that is written down is one the next person can
#  find.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Python: the environment, and what the interpreter writes as it runs ------------------------
#  `venv/` is created by `start.bat` and installed from `backend/requirements.txt`, which *is*
#  tracked. Committing the environment would put another machine's binaries in the history and
#  still not be the environment anyone else needs.
venv/
.venv/
env/
ENV/
#  Bytecode and the interpreter's own output: regenerated on import, and they embed absolute paths.
#  `[cod]` covers `.pyd` as well, the Windows extension modules.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so
.Python

# --- Python packaging, if the engine is ever built into one ------------------------------------
#  This project has no `setup.py` and no `pyproject.toml` -- `start.bat` runs the engine from source
#  -- so the setuptools era's output is deliberately *not* listed here: `develop-eggs/`, `eggs/`,
#  `.eggs/`, `sdist/`, `wheels/`, `share/python-wheels/`, `*.egg`, `MANIFEST` and the rest of the
#  block every Python template carries. None of it can appear, and a list of things that cannot
#  happen is how a file like this stops being read. What is left is what could:
#
#  `/build/` is where `python -m build` writes, anchored at the root so that a *source* folder
#  called `build` anywhere else is not swallowed by it. `*.egg-info/` is what `pip install -e .`
#  leaves. `pip-log.txt` is written by pip when it is told to log.
/build/
*.egg-info/
pip-log.txt

# --- Tests and checkers: what running them leaves behind ---------------------------------------
#  `backend/tests` and `specs/features` are source and are tracked; these are the caches and the
#  reports. Reports are ignored rather than committed because they describe one machine's run.
#
#  The engine is checked with pytest and pyflakes -- `start.bat` runs the first of those on every
#  launch -- so the entry here is pytest's. The other frameworks' caches are deliberately *not*
#  listed: `.mypy_cache/`, `.ruff_cache/`, `.tox/`, `.nox/`, `.hypothesis/`, `nosetests.xml`,
#  `.dmypy.json` and `.python-version` are in every Python template and none of them can appear in
#  a project that does not run those tools. If one is added later, its line goes here, which is
#  written down so that nobody has to wonder whether it was an oversight.
.pytest_cache/
#  Coverage output is kept although nothing produces it today: it is an artefact by nature, it is
#  large, and the cost of the four lines is nothing next to committing a `coverage.xml` by accident.
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
coverage.xml

# --- Node and the interface --------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Both dependency trees are installed by `start.bat` from the two tracked lock files, which is
#  what makes an install reproducible. The lock files stay; the trees do not.
node_modules/
frontend/node_modules/
#  `frontend/dist` is the built interface, and the engine serves it to the window. `start.bat`
#  rebuilds it by itself when a source file is newer than the bundle, so it is never stale in a
#  clone for longer than one launch.
frontend/dist/
.vite/
frontend/.vite/
*.tsbuildinfo
.eslintcache

# --- The tools start.bat installs when a machine has none --------------------------------------
#  A portable Node and ffmpeg, and the archives they came from: about a quarter of a gigabyte, put
#  there by `scripts/bootstrap.ps1` for a machine that had neither. Committing it would put
#  somebody else's platform-specific binaries in the history for no gain; running start.bat again
#  reproduces all of it, which is the whole point of that file.
.tools/

# --- Media, and the one accident this repository is most exposed to -----------------------------
#
#  The repository holds no footage and no transcripts, on purpose: not one check in it cuts a
#  media file, so there is nothing here that needs one and nothing that would go stale if one
#  changed. Verified, not assumed -- every tracked file is source:
#
#      .py .ts .tsx .css .html .json .ini .md .feature .ps1 .cjs .bat .woff2 .txt
#
#  Which makes any media file in this folder an accident, and the accident is large. The engine
#  writes a segment, and its transcript, *beside the source*: point it at a master that happens to
#  live inside this checkout and a 7 GB take plus its captions land in the working tree, one
#  `git add -A` from a history nobody can shrink. So the extensions are refused up front.
#
#  If a fixture is ever genuinely wanted, this is where to write the `!` exception, and the point of
#  naming it here is that it becomes a decision somebody made rather than an accident.
*.mp4
*.mov
*.mkv
*.m4v
*.avi
*.webm
*.wmv
*.mpg
*.mpeg
*.vob
*.m2ts
*.3gp
#  `.ts` and `.mts` are both MPEG transport streams and both TypeScript source extensions, and this
#  project writes TypeScript. Neither is listed: a rule for either would ignore the interface's own
#  source, silently, from the first commit after it was added -- and a `.mts` file added later by
#  somebody who had not read this far would be ignored for reasons nothing on screen explains.
#  `.m2ts` above is the unambiguous form, and a bare transport stream is not a name this product
#  writes.
#
#  Captions travel with a segment, so they are refused with it: the whole point of naming a segment
#  is that `clip.mp4` and `clip.srt` arrive as a pair.
*.srt
*.vtt
*.ass
*.ssa
*.sub
#  Sound, for the same reason: an audio master or a delivered stem dropped here is the same
#  accident in a smaller box.
*.wav
*.mp3
*.m4a
*.aac
*.flac
*.ogg
#  A folder to put any of it in deliberately, so the rules above are for the accidental case.
/media/
/footage/

#  What a cut writes beside its source when it is *not* named: `default_output()` calls it
#  `<stem> HH.MM.SS.FF-HH.MM.SS.FF` with the source's own extension. Kept as well as the rules
#  above, because it says which files those are without consulting the extension list -- and a named
#  segment can be called anything, which no pattern can describe, so the extensions are what
#  actually do the work.
* [0-9][0-9].[0-9][0-9].[0-9][0-9].[0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9].[0-9][0-9].[0-9][0-9].[0-9][0-9].*

#  Where the engine's temporary pieces go, and why they are not listed above: `trim()` writes the
#  head, the body, the tail and the sound into a directory under the system temp folder
#  (`tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="thetrimmer-")`), joins them there and removes it. A cut leaves
#  nothing behind in the working tree, so there is nothing here to ignore for it.

# --- Packaging output --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  `electron-builder` writes here -- `build.directories.output` in package.json -- for the `--dir`
#  form and the installer alike: the unpacked app, the installer, its blockmap and the channel
#  files generated beside them.
/dist/
/release/
/out/
*.blockmap
builder-effective-config.yaml
electron-builder.env

# --- Anything that could carry a secret --------------------------------------------------------
#  Nothing in this project needs a file like these today. That is the reason to refuse them now
#  rather than after somebody adds a proxy password or a signing key to make one machine work: the
#  repository is public, and a secret committed once is public for good, because removing it in a
#  later commit does not remove it from the history.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.pfx
*.p12
*.keystore
secrets.json

# --- The application's own working files -------------------------------------------------------
#  A project file is a person's list of marks and belongs to them rather than to the repository.
#  The journal beside it is written while a project is open.
*.trimmerproj
*.trimmerproj-journal
/scratch/
*.log
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
pnpm-debug.log*

# --- Leftovers ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  A patch that did not apply, a merge that was abandoned, an editor's backup, a temporary file.
#  `nul` is the Windows one worth naming: `> nul` where `>nul` was meant creates a file called
#  `nul`, which no Windows program can then open or delete by name.
*.orig
*.rej
*.bak
*.tmp
*.temp
nul

# --- Editors -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
*.sublime-workspace
*.sublime-project
.project
.classpath
.settings/

# --- Operating system --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  `Thumbs.db` and `desktop.ini` are written into any folder Windows has been asked to draw,
#  including this one, just by looking at it.
.DS_Store
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
Desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/
*.stackdump
